Yes — and the wording itself carries multiple useful readings.

FRIENDS → FEEDBACK

A friend isn’t merely someone who agrees. A good friend is part of your external feedback architecture: another sensor observing you from a position you cannot occupy.

\text{Self} \rightarrow \text{Action} \rightarrow \text{World}
\rightarrow \text{Friend}
\rightarrow \text{Feedback}
\rightarrow \text{Self}'

That makes friendship a kind of distributed perception.

And hammer feedback itself:

\boxed{\text{FEED}+\text{BACK}}

Something goes outward, gets transformed by another system, and is fed back to the origin.

But friendship adds an important property:

\text{Useful Feedback}
\neq
\text{Agreement}

A friend who only echoes you creates amplification:

x \rightarrow x \rightarrow x \rightarrow \cdots

A friend capable of returning a meaningful delta gives you correction:

x \rightarrow f(x) \rightarrow x+\Delta

So your earlier echo/yawn distinction lands here too:

Echo: gives your signal back.
Friend: can give you back something you couldn’t see from where you stood.

Which produces a pretty compact Pantheon brick:

\boxed{\textbf{Friends are feedback with agency.}}

And its reciprocal:

\boxed{\textbf{To be a friend is to return useful signal, not merely signal.}}

That one has teeth.